{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"PROVEN RCA / CLASSIFICATION (rebar-debug Phase 1, refreshed against origin/main 89128b2e09)\n\nMechanism: linked worktrees have distinct per-worktree git dirs but share one git common dir. Git stores the newest stash at the shared ref `refs/stash`; bare `git stash pop` selects `stash@{0}`, the newest entry in that repository-wide reflog, without recording or checking the creating worktree. Therefore two interleaved push/pop sequences can silently apply each other's changes. This is Git's documented design, not a Git defect.\n\nDiscriminating runtime proof: in isolated `/tmp/rebar-prudent-stash.c7Ipdk`, worktrees A and B reported different `--git-dir` paths but the same `--git-common-dir`. A stashed deletion A, then B stashed deletion B, producing `stash@{0}: B-owned-by-wt-b` and `stash@{1}: A-owned-by-wt-a`. Bare `git stash pop` in A applied B's deletion; bare pop in B then applied A's deletion. Final observable state was `wt-a: D b-owned.txt` and `wt-b: D a-owned.txt`. This confirms shared-ref/top-of-stack selection and rejects both a worktree-local stash-stack hypothesis and a wrong-worktree-path hypothesis. No shared repository stash was mutated.\n\nDefect legitimacy is intentionally split because the ticket combines different decisions:\n\n1. DEFECT / incomplete safety documentation: `AGENTS.md:100-131` explicitly promotes parallel-agent work; `AGENTS.md:217-220` mandates fresh linked worktrees; `Makefile:157-179` makes that workflow one command. The original agent-fitness change `c5d79b58b6` explicitly hardened concurrency and introduced this parallel workflow, and `minor-haste-ogle` establishes non-destructive preservation of work as a safety invariant. Yet the only stash warning is `AGENTS.md:264-267`, narrowly scoped to the shared main checkout; no adjacent guidance explains that stash is repository-global across every linked worktree. That omission permits behavior contrary to the approved safe-parallel-work intent. An adjacent warning plus worktree-local alternatives is a legitimate Phase-2 repair. Closed `sultanlike-doughty-civet` is direct prior art: `AGENTS.md:234-240` already teaches the analogous shared-common-dir hook hazard.\n\n2. INTENDED UPSTREAM BEHAVIOR: the global `refs/stash` stack itself is working as Git defines it. Rebar cannot treat it as a worktree-local facility.\n\n3. DESIGN DECISION / feature request: a mechanical repository-wide `git stash` blocker is not a straightforward hook fix. An isolated probe showed `git config alias.stash ...` cannot shadow Git's built-in `stash`; Git exposes no pre-stash or general pre-command hook. A PATH-level `git` wrapper would be bypassable and would intercept every Git consumer, including rebar internals, so adopting it changes repository tooling architecture and needs an explicit decision rather than automatic bug repair.\n\n4. OPERATIONAL COORDINATION: returning the displaced ADR 0061 work and later deleting its stash/backup is not a code repair. It was deliberately left untouched; cleanup requires confirmation from that work's owner.\n\nSibling sweep: `src/rebar/_store/push.py:203-260` still issues `git stash push`, then bare `git stash pop`; `_resolve_conflicted_pop` also drops the current top entry at `push.py:197-200`. The ticket-store write lock does not serialize this repository-global stack against an agent in another linked worktree. Closed `filmy-basin-chasm` repaired pop-conflict cleanup but retained the assumption that the top stash belongs to rebar. This is a distinct executable sibling hazard and should receive its own Phase-1 proof/design disposition before changing the approved Tier-D stash/merge behavior (`docs/bash-migration.md:261-365`).\n\nShared-common-dir sweep disposition: hooks are already fixed/documented by `sultanlike-doughty-civet`; refs, object storage, and repository config are also shared by design but are not inherently destructive stack operations. The live high-risk stack-like construct found is stash, including the internal `push.py` caller above.\n\nRecommended disposition: proceed automatically only with the adjacent documentation/safe-alternatives defect. Ask the operator to decide separately whether to adopt a Git wrapper or another enforcement architecture, and how to replace/serialize rebar's internal stash dance. Preserve the displaced stash and backup until their owner confirms recovery. No code edit, claim, transition, stash mutation, or cleanup was performed in Phase 1."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786303401060461001,"uuid":"606e3e2c-f5dc-4704-917a-a00eb4c8f471"}